WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.630 --> 00:00:02.950
SPEAKER: Processing
data can be complicated,

00:00:02.950 --> 00:00:04.620
especially when
the volume of data

00:00:04.620 --> 00:00:08.250
is variable, and even more so
when the data is streaming.

00:00:08.250 --> 00:00:10.335
There's lots of it out
there, and you usually

00:00:10.335 --> 00:00:12.960
need to work with it before you
can start to get some value out

00:00:12.960 --> 00:00:13.570
of it.

00:00:13.570 --> 00:00:16.410
But how do you work
with data efficiently,

00:00:16.410 --> 00:00:18.300
and how do you scale it?

00:00:18.300 --> 00:00:19.890
In these videos,
we're going to talk

00:00:19.890 --> 00:00:22.980
about Apache Beam, a
flexible and open source

00:00:22.980 --> 00:00:24.570
tool for processing data.

00:00:24.570 --> 00:00:28.290
Specifically, it's built to
unify batch and streaming

00:00:28.290 --> 00:00:30.240
parallel data processing.

00:00:30.240 --> 00:00:33.630
Let's start by talking about
why Beam exists and then

00:00:33.630 --> 00:00:35.436
go through how it works.

00:00:35.436 --> 00:00:38.838
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:43.700 --> 00:00:46.550
You may be familiar with
the original MapReduce paper

00:00:46.550 --> 00:00:48.260
from back in 2004.

00:00:48.260 --> 00:00:50.540
Over the years, the
usage of MapReduce

00:00:50.540 --> 00:00:52.670
grew exponentially in Google.

00:00:52.670 --> 00:00:54.680
But people started
running into limitations

00:00:54.680 --> 00:00:56.870
with complex
multi-stage pipelines.

00:00:56.870 --> 00:00:59.180
The answer to this
was FlumeJava,

00:00:59.180 --> 00:01:02.450
which was released as
a paper around 2011.

00:01:02.450 --> 00:01:04.819
There was also a need
for accurate, low

00:01:04.819 --> 00:01:08.930
latency scaled pipelines,
which the MillWheel focused on.

00:01:08.930 --> 00:01:11.180
Shortly after, these
two technologies

00:01:11.180 --> 00:01:15.650
came together to become the
foundations for Apache Beam.

00:01:15.650 --> 00:01:18.200
Beam is designed to
allow folks the freedom

00:01:18.200 --> 00:01:21.170
to compose highly expressive
batch or streaming

00:01:21.170 --> 00:01:23.330
pipelines with the
language of their choice

00:01:23.330 --> 00:01:26.900
and to run those pipelines on
a number of different runners.

00:01:26.900 --> 00:01:31.940
Supported languages include
Java, Python, Go, and SQL.

00:01:31.940 --> 00:01:34.130
And you can also
choose the runner,

00:01:34.130 --> 00:01:36.950
which is where the pipeline
will actually be executed.

00:01:36.950 --> 00:01:39.410
For these videos, we'll
be using Google Cloud Data

00:01:39.410 --> 00:01:41.630
Flow as our example runner.

00:01:41.630 --> 00:01:45.260
The flexibility of Beam goes
beyond programming languages.

00:01:45.260 --> 00:01:47.900
It also covers
lots of use cases.

00:01:47.900 --> 00:01:50.120
You can set up pipelines
to deal with everything

00:01:50.120 --> 00:01:54.230
from simple transportation,
which mainly involves ingestion

00:01:54.230 --> 00:01:57.530
and simple transformations,
all the way to creating

00:01:57.530 --> 00:02:00.650
a continuous
intelligence solution.

00:02:00.650 --> 00:02:03.680
As you can probably see,
portability and scale

00:02:03.680 --> 00:02:06.120
are key parts of Apache Beam.

00:02:06.120 --> 00:02:08.509
So let's look
deeper into how Beam

00:02:08.509 --> 00:02:12.830
works by going over pipelines
and a few key concepts.

00:02:12.830 --> 00:02:15.020
Pipelines are basically
the description

00:02:15.020 --> 00:02:16.700
of how you'll be
working with data.

00:02:16.700 --> 00:02:20.510
Pipelines are essentially made
up of one or more data sources,

00:02:20.510 --> 00:02:23.780
a collection of transformations,
and then one or more places

00:02:23.780 --> 00:02:25.340
for the data to end up.

00:02:25.340 --> 00:02:29.660
This pipeline also represents
a directed, acyclic graph.

00:02:29.660 --> 00:02:31.670
Let's talk about some
of the core primitives

00:02:31.670 --> 00:02:33.230
that make up a pipeline.

00:02:33.230 --> 00:02:37.640
A PCollection is an immutable,
unordered collection of values.

00:02:37.640 --> 00:02:41.660
And it can contain a bounded or
unbounded number of elements.

00:02:41.660 --> 00:02:45.890
Beam uses PCollection objects
as inputs and outputs,

00:02:45.890 --> 00:02:48.500
operating on them
with transforms.

00:02:48.500 --> 00:02:51.950
A PTransform is an
operation in a pipeline.

00:02:51.950 --> 00:02:54.590
These transforms are
basically functions

00:02:54.590 --> 00:02:58.910
applied to each element of one
or more input PCollections.

00:02:58.910 --> 00:03:01.000
Depending on the
runner, multiple workers

00:03:01.000 --> 00:03:04.910
that cause a cluster may
execute the code in parallel.

00:03:04.910 --> 00:03:07.970
Ultimately, the transform
produces one or more output

00:03:07.970 --> 00:03:11.030
PCollections after the
function is applied.

00:03:11.030 --> 00:03:14.600
Since PTransforms input
and output PCollections,

00:03:14.600 --> 00:03:17.510
you can chain them together
to make a pipeline.

00:03:17.510 --> 00:03:19.670
And then there's
the pipeline itself.

00:03:19.670 --> 00:03:21.890
The pipeline object
helps describe

00:03:21.890 --> 00:03:25.310
the relationship between
PCollections and PTransforms.

00:03:25.310 --> 00:03:28.740
Since it can support one
or more inputs and outputs,

00:03:28.740 --> 00:03:30.770
you can even chain
together pipelines

00:03:30.770 --> 00:03:34.850
to support whatever kind of
business logic you might need.

00:03:34.850 --> 00:03:38.210
As I said before, data can
be tricky to deal with.

00:03:38.210 --> 00:03:40.460
These concepts might seem
pretty straightforward

00:03:40.460 --> 00:03:43.760
when looking at batch data,
but streaming event data

00:03:43.760 --> 00:03:45.830
can introduce more complexity.

00:03:45.830 --> 00:03:49.580
Thankfully, Beam is designed to
handle both batch and streaming

00:03:49.580 --> 00:03:52.970
data processing with ease thanks
to the ability to group data

00:03:52.970 --> 00:03:55.100
using windows.

00:03:55.100 --> 00:03:57.350
A window subdivides
a PCollection

00:03:57.350 --> 00:04:00.980
by using the timestamps of
each individual element.

00:04:00.980 --> 00:04:02.990
There is multiple
types of windows

00:04:02.990 --> 00:04:04.790
that can be used
to group elements,

00:04:04.790 --> 00:04:08.810
such as fixed windows or
session-based windows based

00:04:08.810 --> 00:04:10.640
on clusters of events.

00:04:10.640 --> 00:04:12.980
It makes sense that
an event probably

00:04:12.980 --> 00:04:14.900
has a timestamp of
when it happened,

00:04:14.900 --> 00:04:17.540
but that doesn't necessarily
mean that's when your data

00:04:17.540 --> 00:04:20.240
processing receives the event.

00:04:20.240 --> 00:04:24.500
Think about a mobile device that
temporarily loses connection.

00:04:24.500 --> 00:04:27.380
You'll know the timestamp
of when the event happens,

00:04:27.380 --> 00:04:29.540
like the different
actions a user might take

00:04:29.540 --> 00:04:31.430
while playing a game offline.

00:04:31.430 --> 00:04:33.470
But when the device
gets a connection

00:04:33.470 --> 00:04:36.330
and sends all of those
events over at once,

00:04:36.330 --> 00:04:39.200
you may see a significant
difference in the timestamps.

00:04:39.200 --> 00:04:44.210
That's why we separate event
time from processing time.

00:04:44.210 --> 00:04:46.820
If you were to use the
processing time as a window,

00:04:46.820 --> 00:04:49.460
you might end up with
events in the wrong area,

00:04:49.460 --> 00:04:52.430
and your groups could
be mixing up events.

00:04:52.430 --> 00:04:55.220
Grouping by event time
gives you the right groups.

00:04:55.220 --> 00:04:57.260
But that introduces
another question.

00:04:57.260 --> 00:05:01.820
How do when you've received
all of the data for a window?

00:05:01.820 --> 00:05:05.030
Handling late data is done
with triggers and watermarks,

00:05:05.030 --> 00:05:08.490
but we'll talk about how that
works in an upcoming video.

00:05:08.490 --> 00:05:11.930
So today, we covered a brief
introduction to Apache Beam

00:05:11.930 --> 00:05:14.480
and started to go over
some of the key concepts.

00:05:14.480 --> 00:05:16.670
There's plenty more to cover.

00:05:16.670 --> 00:05:19.070
And in the next video,
we'll go through an example

00:05:19.070 --> 00:05:21.410
of putting Beam to the test.

00:05:21.410 --> 00:05:23.480
If you'd like to learn
more, check out the links

00:05:23.480 --> 00:05:24.840
in the description below.

00:05:24.840 --> 00:05:27.170
And as always, thank
you for watching.

00:05:27.170 --> 00:05:30.520
[MUSIC PLAYING]

